From ea6aa667dee91b71eeea1e6dc5cf86b6780c25c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 00:50:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms, nvidia-drm: HDR10+ through the generic InfoFrame slot

HDR10+ needs its vendor InfoFrame (IEEE OUI 90-84-8B, the metadata of each
frame) next to the HDR one. Hades' 615 sends it through the generic slot of
Turing, Ampere and Ada (nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9020); 580.196.07 has no
HDR10+ at all. The GPUs of this driver have that slot free (the previous
change), so they can carry it too.

A new connector property of nvidia-drm, HDR10PLUS_VSIF, takes the 27 bytes
of the HDR10+ InfoFrame of a frame (OUI first), as on 615: not with
NV_HDMI_VSIF_METADATA, nor in an HDMI 3D frame packed mode. It is attached
where NVKMS says it can go out (getHdr10PlusSupported() of KAPI: a display
driven as HDMI, through an HDMI connector or the TMDS output of a dual-mode
DisplayPort one, on a GPU whose generic slot is free).

It changes with every frame, and the ioctl API of NVKMS has no room for it in
a flip: KAPI passes it with a mode set (NVKMS_HADES_IOCTL_SET_MODE), and when
it changes alone with NVKMS_HADES_IOCTL_HDR10PLUS, kernel clients only,
right before the flip of that frame (tested with a flip that only tests,
written with one that commits). That ioctl writes only the generic slot, and
keeps the InfoFrame with the state of the head, so that a later InfoFrame
update sends it again. As on 615, the write is not synchronized with the
flip (InfoFrames are not on these GPUs): the packet goes out from the
vertical blanking where it is written.

Refused, and said in the kernel log ("Hades: the HDR10+ InfoFrame of head N
is refused: ..."), on a display not driven as HDMI, without a free generic
slot, with Dolby Vision, or while the generic slot carries a vendor
InfoFrame (3D with HDR); the client learns it from the failed commit. The
console drops it with the Dolby VSIF.
